### F16 additional coverage limitations
- `render_json_contract_test.go`: golden round-trip catches removed/renamed fields present in the fixture, but cannot catch newly added `omitempty` fields or producer logic that ceases populating existing fields. Header claims that any added field/changed output is caught are too broad. Add producer-path fixtures for populated optional fields when changing this contract.
- `missing_git_diagnosis_test.go`: comment says all four gates are tested, table contains only path and refresh.
- `live_e2e_test.go`: opt-in live runner truncates arbitrary failure output to 2,000 bytes but does not redact it; the header's claim that output never holds transcript content/credentials is not established by truncation. Diagnostics also print raw log tails. Not run: it invokes real agents. Its timeout kills only the immediate process, not descendants; diagnostics have no timeout.

### F16 further assertion evidence
- `mcp_transport_truth_test.go`, `TestTraceContainmentHoldsForAnUnboundServer` invokes the path helper with an already resolved root. It cannot catch the caller forgetting to resolve an unbound server; the comment claims protection of precisely that wiring regression. Exercise runMCP without RepoRoot from a temporary repository.
- `setup_spawn_unix_test.go`: the own-session test checks process-group IDs, which establishes a separate process group but not independently a new session (setpgid also satisfies it). Its cleanup kills the shell PID only, potentially leaving the `sleep 30` descendant.
- `repo_key_guard_test.go` retains a comment saying the shared guard still fails open for bare `..`; the inspected dotdot regression tests establish that this is historical, not current.
- `seed_eval_experiment_test.go` has the same empty-task/negative-K/non-observed-result denominator limitations as the chunk experiment, and builds an unused factByID map. These are opt-in experiment quality issues, not default test failures.

### F16/F7 remaining rendering/fuzz test notes
`transcript_fuzz_test.go` claims to assert never-hang/never-unbounded-allocation, but has no allocation assertion; its context timeout only works when the scanner cooperates. Digest determinism and line-range properties are actually asserted. `unrunnable_remedy_test.go` checks rule order by inert condition labels, so swapping predicates while keeping labels passes that particular test; outcome cases are the meaningful protection. `setup_render_test.go` claims observer notifications happen as production phases land, but the test manually invokes the observer and cannot prove orchestration timing. These are overclaims of coverage, not failures of the exercised properties.

### F60/F78 existing tests explain missed cases
`setup_pr89_test.go` retries a contended distill only after changing the fingerprint from fp-0 to fp-1; it therefore misses F60, where unchanged work is never retried. `setup_watchplan_test.go` explicitly expects both auto and none to be omitted from the plan. The accompanying rationale covers unresolved auto-detection (an agent might be installed later), but does not distinguish the user's explicit `--agent none` choice established in F78.

### F16 service test gaps
`setup_daemon_concurrency_test.go` concurrent registration test never asserts the number of inspect/install events; no events can vacuously pass. Its uninstall/install test exercises only the shared lock helper, discards its errors, and never calls either operation. `viz_test.go` middleware-to-mux test wraps the mux in the middleware itself, so cannot detect production startup omitting the middleware. Header presence is not a full no-egress proof.

### F16 direct tautology and export test mismatch
`review_regressions_test.go`, `TestFailedVectorSyncDoesNotSelfRelaunch`, assigns VectorContinue=false and immediately asserts it is false; it never invokes a failed sync or the relaunch scheduler. The later add/serialization checks are real but do not establish the headline scheduling property. In the same file `TestPartialTranscriptFailureDoesNotDeleteExportedTranscripts` intentionally invokes cleanup and asserts the retained file was deleted. This demonstrates why the caller must gate cleanup, but does not test that it actually does. It also does not exercise the earlier metadata-selection failure confirmed in F80.

### Additional F7/F16 test audit evidence
- `internal/cli/workspace_multirepo_test.go`: the no-scan review fixture skips if the product scans files, masking fixture/product drift; fail or construct the intended scenario explicitly. Absence of lock files in the forged-key fixture alone does not prove the foreign store was never read.
